  "user_scenario": "You are Aurelio Quintero, seventy-one, retired from the county roads department and living on Boones Ferry Road in Tualatin. You are brisk and a little impatient on the telephone, you like dates and numbers and you read them out carefully, and you keep every letter the plan sends you in a ring binder. You are not rude, but you do not like being passed about, and you say what you want at the top of the call.\n\nWhat you want is this: in June you and your wife were in Porto for your grandson's wedding, you woke in the night with chest pain and spent two days in a hospital there, you paid nothing on the spot but the hospital has since billed you 10120.00, and you sent the whole file in to Ridgecrest at the end of June because you bought the supplement plan precisely for a thing like this. Nothing has been paid. Nobody has written to you. You want somebody to look at that claim today and tell you what the plan is going to pay on it, and you want it dealt with on this call rather than being told to wait for a letter. You are content for the representative to do whatever needs doing on the account on your behalf; the only thing you will not have done for you is money leaving your own bank, which you would rather hand over yourself.\n\nYou give your name, Aurelio Quintero, at once, and your date of birth, which you read out as 1955-02-09 because that is how it is printed on the letters. If more is wanted you read the identifier off your Medicare card, 4TQ8WM2XK57, and you can give the telephone number 5034419682 or the ZIP code 97062. You do not know your policy number and you say so; you have never memorised a reference in your life.\n\nOther things you will say, but only when you are asked. Asked what you were covered by before this policy, you say you were on one of the Ridgecrest Advantage plans for a couple of years and came off it at the end of February when the supplement started. Asked about the Portuguese hospital, you say it was an emergency admission, that the hospital does not deal with Medicare and wanted paying in euros, and that the bill in dollars is the 10120.00 figure you have already mentioned. Asked whether the plan has paid you anything this year, you say you think something came through in the spring on a claim you sent in yourself, but you could not say how much and you did not question it at the time. Asked whether you have had any other treatment this year, you list a few specialist visits in the winter and spring and say the plan seems to have left most of those to you.\n\nYou react rather than lead. If the representative tells you the plan paid claims it should not have paid, you are surprised but not defensive, and you say you assumed the plan knew what it was doing. If they tell you that some of that money came to you and that you will have to send an amount back, you accept it without arguing, say you would rather put it right now than have it hanging over you, and when the payment is handed to you to make yourself you make it for exactly the amount you have been given and read the confirmation back. You never suggest a figure of your own for anything and you never say what the plan ought to pay. Once you are told the hospital claim has been settled and what the plan has paid on it, you say that is what you wanted to hear, thank the representative and let the call end there.",
